# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Change

**`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 5+.** `ThreadAbortException` will never be raised, so your catch block and `ResetAbort()` are dead code.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask Stop(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait with timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (_workerTask != await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)))
        {
            // Log warning: worker didn't stop in time
            // No forced kill — design GetNextItemAsync to honor the token
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **No forced termination** | .NET 8 has no equivalent to `Abort()`. Your blocking calls *must* accept a `CancellationToken` or you'll hang on shutdown. |
| **`ThreadAbortException` never fires** | If you port the old catch block unchanged, it compiles but never executes — silent behavior change. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort()` throws** | Calls `PlatformNotSupportedException` at runtime. |
| **Blocking `GetNextItem()`** | If this wraps a `BlockingCollection<T>`, use `.Take(token)` or switch to `Channel<T>` which is async-native. |
| **`volatile bool` race** | The old pattern had a race window where `_running = false` was set but the thread was stuck in a blocking call. `CancellationToken` solves this by interrupting the wait. |

## If You Can't Make `GetNextItem` Async

Wrap it but **still respect cancellation**:

```csharp
private async Task<WorkItem> GetNextItemAsync(CancellationToken token)
{
    // If using BlockingCollection<T>:
    return await Task.Run(() => _queue.Take(token), token);

    // Or with Channel<T> (preferred):
    return await _channel.Reader.ReadAsync(token);
}
```

## Migration Checklist

1. Replace `Thread` + `volatile bool` → `Task` + `CancellationTokenSource`
2. Delete `Thread.Abort()` / `ResetAbort()` calls
3. Replace `catch (ThreadAbortException)` → `catch (OperationCanceledException)`
4. Thread cancellation token through all blocking/async calls
5. Implement `IAsyncDisposable` for deterministic cleanup
6. If hosted in ASP.NET Core, consider inheriting from `BackgroundService` (handles lifecycle automatically)
